    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d28">

                <pb facs="0142" n="18" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg18"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A4/a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harvey</hi>)<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d28-f1"/></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-07-08T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 8, 1921—4
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>384. Ascertain informally whether it would be agreeable to the British Government
                    to be invited by this Government to participate in a conference on limitation of
                    armament, the conference to be held in Washington at a mutually convenient time.
                    Similar suggestions will be made to the French, Italian, and Japanese
                    Governments. Until invitation is issued it is desired that the matter shall be
                    held in confidence.</p>
                <p>For your information only: It is probable that formal invitation will not be sent
                    until after Congress passes the naval bill with Borah Resolution.<anchor n="18"
                        xml:id="d28-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d28-f1" n="17" type="source" xml:id="d28-f1-content">
                <p>The same, <hi rendition="#italic">mutatis mutandis</hi>, to the diplomatic
                    representatives in France (no. 330), Italy (no. 121), and Japan (no. 104). (File
                    nos. 500.A4/b, c, d.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d28-f2" n="18" xml:id="d28-f2-content">
